-### Deceptive-NeRF
-Uses an MLP-based NeRF (Nerfacto) as the backbone [S1]. Suitable for scenes where volumetric representation is preferred or when integrating with existing NeRF pipelines [S1]. [Simple-NeRF](7371-simple-nerf.md)
-
-### Deceptive-3DGS
-Uses 3D Gaussian Splatting (Splatfacto) as the backbone [S1]. Suitable for real-time rendering requirements and scenes with complex geometry where Gaussian primitives excel [S1]. [Sparse View and Few-Shot Optimization](14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md)
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Sparse Inputs:** Standard NeRF/3DGS pipelines suffer from severe artifacts and overfitting with sparse inputs [S1].
+*   **Uncertainty Guidance:** Without uncertainty guidance, the diffusion model cannot effectively remove artifacts in coarse renderings, leading to inconsistencies between pseudo-observations and input images [S1]. Ablation shows PSNR drops from 20.44 to 18.61 without uncertainty guidance [S1].
+*   **Initialization:** For 3DGS, random initialization is used instead of Colmap point clouds [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Nearly tenfold faster than methods using diffusion models as per-step regularizers (e.g., SDS) because the diffusion model is inferred only during the pseudo-observation generation phase, not at every optimization step [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Nearly tenfold faster than methods using diffusion models as per-step regularizers/scorers [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   On Hypersim dataset with 10 input views: PSNR 20.44, SSIM 0.748, LPIPS 0.173, Training Time 17min, Rendering FPS 5.3 [S1].
-    *   Comparison with "Regularizer" approach on Hypersim: Regularizer achieved PSNR 19.31, SSIM 0.710, LPIPS 0.253, Training Time 2h40min, Rendering FPS 1.1 [S1].
+    *   On Hypersim dataset with 10 input views:
+        *   Deceptive-NeRF ("View Densifier"): PSNR 20.44, SSIM 0.748, LPIPS 0.173, Training Time 17min, Rendering FPS 5.3 [S1].
+        *   Regularizer variant: PSNR 19.31, SSIM 0.710, LPIPS 0.253, Training Time 2h40min, Rendering FPS 1.1 [S1].
     *   On DTU dataset scan 65 with 9 input views: Chamfer Distance 1.86 (vs NeRF 3.75, FreeNeRF 3.63, DiffusioNeRF 1.93) [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** The method scales with the number of input views, showing consistent improvement as views increase from 3 to 10 on Hypersim [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High-quality reconstruction in sparse-view settings; fast training and rendering compared to diffusion-regularized methods; effective super-resolution capabilities [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the pre-trained diffusion model; requires careful tuning of the uncertainty measure; performance may degrade with extremely sparse inputs (<3 views) [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required from a limited set of input images (3-10 views) and computational resources for per-step diffusion inference are limited [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Works effectively with as few as 3 input images [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High-quality sparse-view reconstruction; significantly faster training and rendering compared to regularizer-based diffusion methods; effective at removing artifacts in coarse renderings.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-trained 2D diffusion model; performance depends on the quality of the coarse initial reconstruction.
+*   **When to use:** Sparse-view reconstruction scenarios where standard NeRF/3DGS fail due to overfitting or artifacts.
+*   **When not to use:** When computational resources for diffusion model inference are extremely limited (though less than regularizer methods, it still requires inference); when dense input views are available (standard methods may suffice).
 
 ## Background
-Deceptive-NeRF/3DGS was proposed by X. Liu et al. in the paper "Deceptive-NeRF/3DGS: Diffusion-Generated Pseudo-Observations for High-Quality Sparse-View Reconstruction" [S1]. The method addresses the limitation of NeRF and 3DGS requiring large numbers of training views, which is often impractical for casual data collection using mobile devices [S1]. It leverages the prior knowledge embedded in large-scale 2D diffusion models to generate pseudo-observations, effectively "deceiving" the reconstruction algorithm into believing it has more data than it does [S1]. The approach was validated on Hypersim, mip-NeRF 360, and DTU datasets [S1].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed by X. Liu et al. in "Deceptive-NeRF/3DGS: Diffusion-Generated Pseudo-Observations for High-Quality Sparse-View Reconstruction" [S1].
+*   **History:** The method addresses the limitation of NeRF and 3DGS requiring large numbers of training views [S1]. It builds on the idea of using diffusion priors for 3D reconstruction but shifts from regularization to view densification [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used on Hypersim dataset [47], mip-NeRF 360 dataset [4], and DTU dataset [S1].
 
